When I say bulky, I mean bulky to read, not as in huge. Sorry
for the clarification. You are very much correct, size dont matter
with bone files. (indeed, with Hex instead of base64 encoding,
the main bulk of the data of the other file format probably would
be longer)

Call it: idiot n00b manuver though :-p Anyway, I don't see XML
as a solution to the main issue at hand here anyway; when someone
makes a bone-incompadible change, they don't have to change the
loader/saver of the bone files, and it will still work 100% fine. The
only
issue is of course, incompadible bone files.

File format is the least of the issue here. What we need first is to
know _what_ to save, not how to save, if you get my drift. AKA: We
can throw XML in there and encode everything in Base64 instead of
binary, but the incompadible bone file problem will still be there :-p

A more practical example, just forcing the tags to be on everything,
so the file looks like this:

<version>
<version_number> 3.4.3 </version_number>
<version_features> 353 </version_features>
<etc. etc.></etc. etc.>
</version>

Still isn't enough for portability sake. (version features probably
should be enumerated like </sink> and </RLEcomp> and so forth, not
just the value for )

In any case; I think we are looking at the problem incorrectly.
Regardless
of how you store the data, you'll need a way that portably enumerates
features like </sink> and so forth, while somehow still maintaining a
way to make bone-incompadible changes without changing the makedef
file or saver/loader :-/

